[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186412202 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,4\\le z\\le 8-x,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-\\frac{x}{2}\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281288 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le y\\le 2z\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281287 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le 2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281300 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a7/a7261baec0c907779f5b1f5ff669ca826d4b93c5dd1b9a389e0989fe34756a6e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65c61ad7dc2964cd0de9a6bbdf259fcf3388e25da4eaabf155d58ff0c05c8d55.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-2y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-2y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-2y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4-x-2y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4-x)y-{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-1\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(6-2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412209 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c4/c4cf12ec9b53282620c1730f824c96d98e0b9dbabee0478bf258cf6c2741da81.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b497e6ef73b5b16e11baa08819e9628d3b5e1f2ee4d05fbafec48f73e087cbf1.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=1+y+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+y+z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(1+y)z+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}+y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412194 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7c1738e97bc16a32f86d6901136afc4d9e288035d170fcd640db340ad36d19c5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(y),$ we substitute $x=1$ into the equation $x=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z:$\n\n$$\n1=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\,⟹\\,z=2-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea8b8397163309a11aa478fcc21ab53ee293c19f0d77a93b5c3d3472bb0eba70.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3-z/2}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2-y/3-z/2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}(2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2})-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(2-\\frac{y}{3})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2-2y/3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}((2-\\frac{y}{3})(2-\\frac{2y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}{(2-\\frac{2y}{3})}^{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})((2-\\frac{y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}(2-\\frac{2y}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{6})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3-\\frac{4y}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[3y-\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{27}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-\\frac{2(3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{27})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412207 :question/worked-solution "![](images/59/599b5392add89a6a22eb6c2232fedbdd26704ebf849bb05d7de52a6d81dcfb6a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7c6fad45d9d85bad5ca16a2000e4d44a1739ab70797e41ae94945a4ffb02ef.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/2-z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-x/2-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3-\\frac{x}{2}-z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3-\\frac{x}{2})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3-\\frac{x}{2})(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8-2)-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281292 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(4(2)-\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}))-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412193 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[x]_{x=-{z}^{2}}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-(-{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[z\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-1}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-(-1))\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\sqrt{y}+3\\,dy \\\\  & ={[2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+3y]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}+3(1)-0 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281293 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,1-y\\le x\\le y+1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{y+1}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=1-y}^{x=y+1}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y+1-(1-y)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129703 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412199 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le x+y+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x+y+2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=x+y+2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+y+2\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+2+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412206 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9-4{y}^{2} \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 9-4{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{9-4{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-4{y}^{2})-(5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}\\,9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =27-27{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412196 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}^{4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-(2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le x\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235411 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}^{3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})-(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})+(1-2{x}^{2})+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-8{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235409 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =1-2{z}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1-2{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the elipse $4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{4{x}^{2}}^{1-2{z}^{2}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{z}^{2})-(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}^{\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}\\,1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-8{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186235407 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the circle ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{y}^{2})-(3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}^{\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}\\,1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-6{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186412202 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,4\\le z\\le 8-x,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-\\frac{x}{2}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186281288 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le y\\le 2z\\}.\n$$"] [17592186281287 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le 2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\}.\n$$"] [17592186281300 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a7/a7261baec0c907779f5b1f5ff669ca826d4b93c5dd1b9a389e0989fe34756a6e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65c61ad7dc2964cd0de9a6bbdf259fcf3388e25da4eaabf155d58ff0c05c8d55.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-2y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-2y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-2y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4-x-2y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4-x)y-{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-1\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(6-2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412209 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c4/c4cf12ec9b53282620c1730f824c96d98e0b9dbabee0478bf258cf6c2741da81.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b497e6ef73b5b16e11baa08819e9628d3b5e1f2ee4d05fbafec48f73e087cbf1.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=1+y+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+y+z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(1+y)z+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}+y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412194 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7c1738e97bc16a32f86d6901136afc4d9e288035d170fcd640db340ad36d19c5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(y),$ we substitute $x=1$ into the equation $x=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z:$\n\n$$\n1=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\,⟹\\,z=2-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea8b8397163309a11aa478fcc21ab53ee293c19f0d77a93b5c3d3472bb0eba70.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3-z/2}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2-y/3-z/2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}(2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2})-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(2-\\frac{y}{3})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2-2y/3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}((2-\\frac{y}{3})(2-\\frac{2y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}{(2-\\frac{2y}{3})}^{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})((2-\\frac{y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}(2-\\frac{2y}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{6})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3-\\frac{4y}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[3y-\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{27}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-\\frac{2(3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{27})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412207 :question/worked-solution "![](images/59/599b5392add89a6a22eb6c2232fedbdd26704ebf849bb05d7de52a6d81dcfb6a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7c6fad45d9d85bad5ca16a2000e4d44a1739ab70797e41ae94945a4ffb02ef.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/2-z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-x/2-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3-\\frac{x}{2}-z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3-\\frac{x}{2})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3-\\frac{x}{2})(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8-2)-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281292 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(4(2)-\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}))-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412193 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[x]_{x=-{z}^{2}}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-(-{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[z\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-1}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-(-1))\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\sqrt{y}+3\\,dy \\\\  & ={[2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+3y]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}+3(1)-0 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281293 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,1-y\\le x\\le y+1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{y+1}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=1-y}^{x=y+1}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y+1-(1-y)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129703 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412199 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le x+y+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x+y+2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=x+y+2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+y+2\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+2+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412206 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9-4{y}^{2} \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 9-4{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{9-4{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-4{y}^{2})-(5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}\\,9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =27-27{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412196 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}^{4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-(2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le x\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235411 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}^{3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})-(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})+(1-2{x}^{2})+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-8{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235409 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =1-2{z}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1-2{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the elipse $4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{4{x}^{2}}^{1-2{z}^{2}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{z}^{2})-(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}^{\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}\\,1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-8{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235407 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the circle ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{y}^{2})-(3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}^{\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}\\,1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-6{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "retractions" [[17592186412202 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,4\\le z\\le 8-x,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-\\frac{x}{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186281288 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le y\\le 2z}.\n$$"] [17592186281287 :question/problem "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le 2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$"] [17592186281300 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a7/a7261baec0c907779f5b1f5ff669ca826d4b93c5dd1b9a389e0989fe34756a6e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65c61ad7dc2964cd0de9a6bbdf259fcf3388e25da4eaabf155d58ff0c05c8d55.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-2y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-2y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-2y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4-x-2y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4-x)y-{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-1\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(6-2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412209 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c4/c4cf12ec9b53282620c1730f824c96d98e0b9dbabee0478bf258cf6c2741da81.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b497e6ef73b5b16e11baa08819e9628d3b5e1f2ee4d05fbafec48f73e087cbf1.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 1+y+z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+y+z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=0}^{x=1+y+z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+y+z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(1+y)z+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}+y\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412194 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7c1738e97bc16a32f86d6901136afc4d9e288035d170fcd640db340ad36d19c5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(y),$ we substitute $x=1$ into the equation $x=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z:$\n\n$$\n1=2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z\\,⟹\\,z=2-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea8b8397163309a11aa478fcc21ab53ee293c19f0d77a93b5c3d3472bb0eba70.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{2}{3}y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{1}{3}y-\\frac{1}{2}z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y/3-z/2}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2-y/3-z/2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}(2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2})-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2y/3}2-\\frac{y}{3}-\\frac{z}{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(2-\\frac{y}{3})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2-2y/3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}((2-\\frac{y}{3})(2-\\frac{2y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}{(2-\\frac{2y}{3})}^{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})((2-\\frac{y}{3})-\\frac{1}{4}(2-\\frac{2y}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2-\\frac{2y}{3})(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{y}{6})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3-\\frac{4y}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\,dy \\\\  & ={[3y-\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{27}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =(3(3)-\\frac{2(3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{27})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412207 :question/worked-solution "![](images/59/599b5392add89a6a22eb6c2232fedbdd26704ebf849bb05d7de52a6d81dcfb6a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e7c6fad45d9d85bad5ca16a2000e4d44a1739ab70797e41ae94945a4ffb02ef.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{2}-z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/2-z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-x/2-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3-\\frac{x}{2}-z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3-\\frac{x}{2}-z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(3-\\frac{x}{2})z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((3-\\frac{x}{2})(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[4x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(8-2)-0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281292 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(4(2)-\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}))-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412193 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[x]_{x=-{z}^{2}}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-(-{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{y}+{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[z\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=-1}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-(-1))\\sqrt{y}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\sqrt{y}+3\\,dy \\\\  & ={[2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}+3y]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}+3(1)-0 \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186281293 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,1-y\\le x\\le y+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{y+1}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=1-y}^{x=y+1}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y+1-(1-y)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129703 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{x=0}^{x=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412199 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le x+y+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x+y+2}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=x+y+2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+y+2\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[(x+2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+2+\\frac{1}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x+\\frac{5}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}x]}_{x=-1}^{x=0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412206 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9-4{y}^{2} \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 9-4{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $9{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{9-4{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-4{y}^{2})-(5{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}}}\\,9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{9-9{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2})+(9-9{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =27-27{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186412196 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}^{4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-(2+3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-1\\le x\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2})+(2-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6-6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235411 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xy$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xy$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le z\\le 3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}^{3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})-(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $y=\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}}\\,2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}})}^{2}+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2{x}^{2})+(1-2{x}^{2})+(2-4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-8{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235409 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $xz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =1-2{z}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $xz$-plane is an ellipse.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1-2{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the elipse $4{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{4{x}^{2}}^{1-2{z}^{2}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{z}^{2})-(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}^{\\sqrt{(1-4{x}^{2})/2}}\\,1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}})}^{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1-4{x}^{2}}{2}+(1-2{z}^{2}-4{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-8{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235407 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine the projection of the intersection of our surfaces onto the $yz$-plane. So, let's find where they intersect.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection in the $yz$-plane is a circle.\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the interior of the circle ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nAs a result, the volume of $R$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{y}^{2})-(3{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the boundary of $D$ consists of \n\n- the lower part $z=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- the upper part $z=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}},$\n\nfor $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}^{\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}}/2}\\,1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}+f & ={(-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-4{y}^{2}})}^{2}+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(1-4{y}^{2})+(1-4{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-6{y}^{2}-4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]]}]